      {
        "attempt": 1,
        "status": "OK",
        "asr_text": "A practical way to choose the setting is to look at the food’s main risk. Apples and pears are good low-humidity examples: they release more ripening gas, and extra airflow can help move it away. Leafy greens, such as kale, and vegetables that lose water easily, such as broccoli, fit the high-humidity side better: keeping more moisture around them can help slow wilting. This gives you a simple rule for foods you may not know well: think about whether you are trying to limit early ripening or prevent moisture loss. That is more useful than memorizing a long list of fruits and vegetables.",
